The `test_zms_read_evil_ate` test case is designed to expose a scenario where a "stale" but valid-looking Allocation Table Entry (ATE) is manually injected into the flash at the current write pointer (`fs->ate_wra`). The test expects `zms_read` to return `-ENOENT` for the corresponding ID, as a `zms_delete` operation for that ID was performed prior to the injection of the stale ATE.

The root cause of the failure is that ZMS's read and write (when `CONFIG_ZMS_NO_DOUBLE_WRITE` is enabled) operations, when `CONFIG_ZMS_LOOKUP_CACHE` is disabled, start their backward scan for the latest ATE from `fs->ate_wra`. The `fs->ate_wra` points to the *next* available slot for writing, which is considered an "uncommitted" area. If an ATE is manually written to this address, it becomes the physically newest entry. Since `zms_find_ate_with_id` (used by `zms_read_hist` and `zms_write`) finds the first valid ATE when scanning backwards, it picks up this manually injected ATE, even though it's logically superseded by a prior `zms_delete` operation.

The fix involves adjusting the starting address for the backward scan in `zms_read_hist` and `zms_write` (when `CONFIG_ZMS_LOOKUP_CACHE` is disabled). Instead of starting the scan from `fs->ate_wra` (the uncommitted slot), it should start from `fs->ate_wra + fs->ate_size`. This effectively skips the uncommitted slot, ensuring that only "committed" ATEs (those written by ZMS's internal logic and thus part of the established log) are considered.

With this change, `zms_find_ate_with_id` will first encounter the `zms_delete` ATE (which has `len=0`) for ID 3. `zms_read_hist` will then correctly interpret this as a deletion and return `-ENOENT`, satisfying the test's assertion.

